# CoreDNS configuration. Committed as-is — no render step.
#
# {$GATEWAY_IP} is CoreDNS's OWN environment-variable syntax: dollar INSIDE the
# braces. It is not shell's ${VAR} and not a typo. The value arrives through the
# `environment:` block in docker-compose.yml. The zone file next door uses shell
# syntax because DNS zone files have no env-var support and must be rendered;
# this file does, so it is not.
#
# Two server blocks, which is the point of this step: one AUTHORITATIVE (it knows
# these names definitively) and one RECURSIVE (it asks someone else). CoreDNS
# matches the most specific zone, so the order here is cosmetic.
#
# Nothing binds an address. The container has its own network namespace and the
# bridge address does not exist inside it — which host interface is exposed is
# decided by the ports mapping in docker-compose.yml, not here.

# Authoritative for lab.test. No forward: if a name is absent from the zone the
# correct answer is NXDOMAIN, not a guess from upstream.
lab.test:53 {
    file /etc/coredns/zones/lab.test.zone
    errors
    log
}

# Everything else. GATEWAY_IP is discovered from the bridge's default route by
# gateway_ip() in lib/common.sh; the public resolver is the fallback for a
# network whose gateway does not serve DNS.
.:53 {
    forward . {$GATEWAY_IP} 1.1.1.1
    cache 30
    errors
    log
}
